---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import katex from "katex";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");

// Typeset at BUILD time, not in the browser: no client JS, and
// throwOnError makes a malformed expression fail the build rather than
// render as red garbage on the live page. Base.astro already loads the CSS.
const M = (src: string, display = true) =>
  katex.renderToString(src, { displayMode: display, throwOnError: true,
                              output: "html" });
---
<Base
  title="mhs — Active fractal"
  description="The unique static frictional solution on a dense cloud of 2056 subparallel slip patches, where the element IS the fracture: which of them slip as the load grows, and what the elastic interaction adds to the loading's own choice."
  current="fractal"
>
  <Fragment slot="sidebar">
    <h1>Active fractal<span class="sub">which fractures actually slip</span></h1>
    <p>
      A dense cloud of small slip patches with nothing prescribed anywhere.
      Each element carries a frictional strength, and slip is permitted only
      where the resolved stress reaches it. Patches that slip change the stress
      on every other patch, so the set that slips is the solution of a coupled
      problem rather than a per-patch test &mdash; and at a fixed load that
      solution is <b>unique</b>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>The element IS the fracture here.</b> There is no fault hierarchy and
      so no inherited coherence: 2,056&nbsp;single triangles of 500&nbsp;m
      edge, <i>&epsilon;</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;50&nbsp;m, each with its own
      orientation, packed at 0.65 of an element width. A rock holds far more
      fractures than take part, and the question is which subset does.
    </p>
    <p>
      The orientations are <b>subparallel</b> &mdash; scattered 20&deg; about
      the Coulomb-optimal plane &mdash; and that is the point. Drawn
      isotropically, neighbours relax each other about as often as they trigger
      and the coupling cancels against itself; aligning them raises the
      triggering budget from 0.58 to 0.76. It also removes the loading's power
      to choose, since every patch is then similarly favoured, which leaves
      <i>position</i> and the elastic interaction as the only things that can
      tell them apart. &mu;<sub>s</sub>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.6 with a 10&nbsp;%
      strength drop, confining stress 150&nbsp;MPa.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <b>This is the experiment the on-fault interaction matrix exists for.</b>
      A stress-driven network needs <i>K<sub>ii</sub></i> &mdash; how much an
      element's own slip relieves its own shear &mdash; and for a classical
      dislocation element that number is undefined on the fault. Mollification
      makes it finite and, measured here, negative on every one of the 2,056
      elements.
    </div>
    <p class="contact">
      The slider is <b>load</b>, not time. Each of the 120 frames is a
      separate <i>static solution</i> &mdash; the unique answer at that
      <i>&lambda;</i> &mdash; but unlike a friction sweep these DO build on one
      another: the load only grows, slip is irreversible, and a frame shows the
      cumulative slip at that load. The events between two frames are what the
      increment colouring shows. Nothing propagates in time; the sequence is a
      path through equilibria, not a rupture.
    </p>
    <p class="contact">
      Arrows at the free surface are the <b>regional stress</b>:
      <span style="color:#c2410c"><b>warm</b></span> inward along the maximum
      compression, <span style="color:#1f6feb"><b>cool</b></span> outward along
      the minimum. Their direction is fixed here and only the magnitude grows,
      to 2.3&nbsp;times the load at which the first patch fails &mdash; which
      is within the point where any patch would go into tension, so every one
      stays in compression at every frame.
    </p>
    <div class="links">
      <div><a href={`${base}concepts/`}>How the kernel works</a></div>
      <div><a href="https://github.com/brendanjmeade/mhs/tree/main/studies/active_fractal">The study code</a></div>
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <div id="viewer">
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="mode"><span>colour</span></label>
      <span data-ctl="play"></span>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="frame"><span>friction</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="theta"><span>stress orientation</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="panel" data-iso-panel>
      <span data-ctl="iso"></span>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="thresh"><span>threshold</span></div>
    </div>
    <div data-canvas class="canvas" aria-label="A three-dimensional cloud of 2056 subparallel slip patches, coloured by cumulative slip as the load increases, with arrows showing the regional stress axes"></div>
    <div data-bar class="bar"></div>
    <p data-status class="status">loading…</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <b>The slip magnitudes are large and are not a prediction.</b> Median
    slip is 12&ndash;16&nbsp;m across the sweep and the maximum reaches
    98&nbsp;m, because the load sits at
    <i>&lambda;</i>/<i>&sigma;<sub>c</sub></i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;3.0 &mdash; a
    differential stress well above any crustal state &mdash; and a static
    solution has no healing, no viscous relaxation and no prior slip history
    to have relieved it. The load was chosen to give a large enough active
    set to measure, which it does. <i>Which</i> elements slip, and how moment
    distributes across them, are the results; how far they slip is what the
    equations give at that load and should be read as such.
  </div>

  <h2>What <i>&lambda;</i> is</h2>

  <p>
    The regional stress is a full tensor growing along a fixed path,
    <i>&sigma;</i>(<i>&lambda;</i>)&nbsp;=&nbsp;<i>&sigma;<sub>c</sub></i>&thinsp;<b>I</b>&nbsp;+&nbsp;<i>&lambda;</i>(<b>D</b>&nbsp;+&nbsp;<i>k</i>&thinsp;<b>I</b>).
    <b>D</b> is a fixed unit tensor giving the <i>shape</i> of the tectonic
    stress &mdash; its orientation and its faulting style &mdash;
    <i>&sigma;<sub>c</sub></i> is the confining pressure, here 150&nbsp;MPa,
    and <i>k</i> is the loading path. Only the magnitude grows during a run;
    the shape never changes, which is what lets a single number stand for the
    whole history.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>D</b>'s eigenvalues are (+1,&nbsp;0,&nbsp;&minus;1), so its maximum shear
    stress is exactly 1 and therefore <b><i>&lambda;</i> is the maximum shear
    stress of the tectonic field, in GPa</b> &mdash; equivalently half the
    differential stress, (<i>&sigma;</i><sub>1</sub>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;<i>&sigma;</i><sub>3</sub>)/2.
    The run starts at <i>&lambda;</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.1175, a tectonic field whose
    maximum shear stress is 118&nbsp;MPa, which is where the first element in
    this network reaches its failure criterion, and ends at 0.4525.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b><i>&lambda;</i> is not time.</b> It carries units of stress, nothing in
    the problem has a rate, and every frame is a static equilibrium. And
    <i>&lambda;</i> alone means nothing: the problem is invariant under scaling
    <i>&lambda;</i> and <i>&sigma;<sub>c</sub></i> together, so the physical
    parameter is the <i>ratio</i> of the two, never either by itself.
  </p>

  <h2>The problem, written out</h2>

  <p>
    Let <i>&delta;<sub>i</sub></i> be the slip on element <i>i</i>, along its
    own slip direction. The shear traction it carries is the regional field
    plus everything every other element's slip transfers to it:
  </p>

  <Fragment set:html={M(String.raw`\tau_i(\lambda,\boldsymbol\delta)\;=\;\underbrace{\lambda\,d_i}_{\text{regional}}\;+\;\underbrace{\textstyle\sum_j K_{ji}\,\delta_j}_{\text{transferred}}`)} />

  <p>
    <i>K</i> is the elastostatic stress-transfer operator &mdash; the one
    <code>mhs</code> assembles &mdash; and <i>d<sub>i</sub></i> is how fast
    the regional field drives element <i>i</i> per unit of load. Its strength
    is Coulomb,
    <i>g<sub>i</sub></i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;<i>&mu;</i>&thinsp;<i>&sigma;</i>'<sub><i>n,i</i></sub>,
    so the <b>yield function</b> is
  </p>

  <Fragment set:html={M(String.raw`F_i \;=\; \tau_i - g_i \;=\; \lambda d_i + \sum_j K_{ji}\delta_j - g_i .`)} />

  <p>
    Friction is then three statements, not one. Slip is non-negative, because
    it runs one way along the driving direction. Traction never exceeds
    strength. And an element slips <i>only</i> while it is exactly at yield:
  </p>

  <Fragment set:html={M(String.raw`\delta_i \ge 0, \qquad F_i \le 0, \qquad \delta_i\,F_i = 0 .`)} />

  <p>
    That last line is the whole of it. Each element is either locked
    (<i>&delta;<sub>i</sub></i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0 and
    <i>F<sub>i</sub></i>&nbsp;&lt;&nbsp;0) or slipping
    (<i>&delta;<sub>i</sub></i>&nbsp;&gt;&nbsp;0 and
    <i>F<sub>i</sub></i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0), and never anything else.
  </p>

  <h3>In standard form</h3>

  <p>
    Write <i>w</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;&minus;<i>F</i> for the margin to failure,
    <b>A</b>&nbsp;=&nbsp;&minus;<b>K</b>, and
    <i>q</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;<i>g</i>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;<i>&lambda;d</i>. The three
    conditions become
  </p>

  <Fragment set:html={M(String.raw`w = \mathbf{A}\boldsymbol\delta + q, \qquad \boldsymbol\delta \ge 0, \qquad w \ge 0, \qquad \boldsymbol\delta^{\mathsf T} w = 0,`)} />

  <p>
    which is the <b>Linear Complementarity Problem</b>
    LCP(<i>q</i>,&nbsp;<b>A</b>) exactly as it is posed in contact mechanics.
    The sign flip is not cosmetic: <b>A</b>'s diagonal is
    <i>A<sub>ii</sub></i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;&minus;<i>K<sub>ii</sub></i>&nbsp;&gt;&nbsp;0,
    because an element's own slip <i>relieves</i> its own shear, and that
    positive diagonal is what everything below rests on.
  </p>

  <h3>Why there is exactly one answer</h3>

  <p>
    LCP(<i>q</i>,&nbsp;<b>A</b>) has a unique solution for <i>every</i>
    <i>q</i> if and only if <b>A</b> is a <b>P-matrix</b> &mdash; all
    principal minors positive (Cottle, Pang &amp; Stone, Thm&nbsp;3.3.7). A
    sufficient condition, and the one actually checkable on an assembled
    operator, is positive definiteness:
  </p>

  <Fragment set:html={M(String.raw`x^{\mathsf T}\mathbf{A}\,x > 0 \;\; \forall x \ne 0 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \tfrac12(\mathbf{A}+\mathbf{A}^{\mathsf T}) \succ 0 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \mathbf{A}\ \text{is a P-matrix.}`)} />

  <p>
    No symmetry is required, which matters here because collocation makes
    <b>A</b> markedly non-symmetric. What is required is that smallest
    eigenvalue of the symmetric part, and it is measured per run rather than
    assumed: <b>2.4</b> on this network, against a matrix whose entries are
    tens of GPa per km of slip.
  </p>

  <h3>How it is solved</h3>

  <p>
    Projected Gauss-Seidel &mdash; one sweep over the elements, each solved
    for its own slip holding the others fixed, then clipped at zero:
  </p>

  <Fragment set:html={M(String.raw`\delta_i \;\longleftarrow\; \max\!\left(0,\; \delta_i - \frac{w_i}{A_{ii}}\right)`)} />

  <p>
    The clip is what makes it a complementarity solve rather than a linear
    one: an element whose update would send it negative is simply locked. The
    convergence test is the <b>natural residual</b>
    max<sub><i>i</i></sub>&nbsp;|min(<i>&delta;<sub>i</sub></i>,&nbsp;<i>w<sub>i</sub></i>)|,
    which is zero at the solution and nowhere else &mdash; a step-size test
    would report success on a solver that had merely slowed down. ~141 sweeps
    cold on 2,056 elements; a handful warm.
  </p>

  <h3>The screening, in the same notation</h3>

  <p>
    Critically-stressed fracture analysis is this problem with
    <b>K</b>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0. The coupling vanishes, every element decouples,
    and the solve collapses to one inequality per element:
  </p>

  <Fragment set:html={M(String.raw`\mathcal{A}_{\text{screen}} = \{\, i \;:\; \lambda d_i \ge g_i \,\}`)} />

  <p>
    No matrix, no iteration. That is the null this study measures against, and
    the comparison is between that set and
    &#123;<i>i</i>&nbsp;:&nbsp;<i>&delta;<sub>i</sub></i>&nbsp;&gt;&nbsp;0&#125;
    from the full solve.
  </p>

  <h3>What is unique and what is not</h3>

  <p>
    The solution <i>&delta;</i>, and therefore the active set, is unique at
    every <i>&lambda;</i> and every <i>&mu;</i>. On a <i>fixed</i> active set
    <i>&#x1D49C;</i> the problem is linear,
    <b>A</b><sub><i>&#x1D49C;&#x1D49C;</i></sub><i>&delta;</i><sub><i>&#x1D49C;</i></sub>&nbsp;=&nbsp;&minus;<i>q</i><sub><i>&#x1D49C;</i></sub>,
    and <i>q</i> is affine in both parameters &mdash; so <i>&delta;</i> is
    <b>piecewise linear</b>, with kinks exactly where the active set changes.
    That is why the counts below move in lumps rather than smoothly.
  </p>

  <p>
    What is <i>not</i> unique is the decomposition of slip into events. That
    requires a strength drop, and a cascade has no unique end state &mdash;
    which is the division this page is organised around.
  </p>

  <h2>The static solution, which is unique</h2>

  <p>
    At one load the quasi-static Coulomb problem is a <b>Linear
    Complementarity Problem</b>. Every element is either locked and below
    strength or slipping and exactly at strength, and nothing else is
    admissible: with <i>w</i> the margin to failure,
    <i>w</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;<b>A</b><i>&delta;</i>&nbsp;+&nbsp;<i>q</i>,
    <i>&delta;</i>&nbsp;&ge;&nbsp;0, <i>w</i>&nbsp;&ge;&nbsp;0,
    <i>&delta;·w</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0, with
    <b>A</b>&nbsp;=&nbsp;&minus;<b>P</b>. When <b>A</b> has positive definite
    symmetric part it is a P-matrix, and an LCP with a P-matrix has
    <b>exactly one solution</b> for every <i>q</i> (Cottle, Pang &amp; Stone,
    Thm 3.3.7).
  </p>

  <p>
    So <i>which faults slip under this stress field</i> has one answer, and it
    depends on no loading path, no update rule and <b>no frictional weakening
    whatsoever</b>. The strength drop above is needed for the separate
    question of how slip arrives &mdash; in what sized jumps &mdash; because
    the stiffness criterion forbids a finite jump without it. Measured here,
    the two static solutions <b>bracket the entire loading history exactly</b>:
  </p>

  <p style="text-align:center">
    <i>A</i>(&mu;<sub>s</sub>)&nbsp;10,822 &nbsp;&sube;&nbsp;
    <i>A</i>(history)&nbsp;10,977 &nbsp;&sube;&nbsp;
    <i>A</i>(&mu;<sub>d</sub>)&nbsp;11,299
  </p>

  <p>
    with zero violations in either direction. The brackets are 477 elements
    apart, 2.3&nbsp;% of the network, so the strength drop does not decide the
    active set &mdash; it only chooses where inside that band the history
    lands. A static solve is also two to three orders of magnitude cheaper:
    ~141 projected Gauss-Seidel sweeps against 165,659 events.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <b>Mollification is what makes this well posed, not merely convenient.</b>
    The uniqueness theorem needs a finite, correctly signed
    <i>P<sub>ii</sub></i>. For a classical dislocation element that number
    does not exist &mdash; the traction kernel is hypersingular on its own
    surface, giving a Hadamard finite part, a collocation-dependent value, or
    a divergence. Mollification smears slip over a width <i>&epsilon;</i> and
    the self-interaction becomes a convergent integral, with
    <i>&epsilon;</i> physical rather than arbitrary: a fault is a gouge zone
    of finite thickness, so <i>P<sub>ii</sub></i> is the self-stiffness of a
    slip patch of that width. Without it the theorem has no matrix to act on.
  </div>

  <p>
    Two things are <i>measured</i> rather than guaranteed. The continuum
    hypersingular operator is coercive for a single crack, but a
    <i>collocation</i> discretization on a network of arbitrary orientations
    and near-contacts does not inherit that &mdash; ours did not, the first
    time, with one negative eigenvalue out of 2,826 traceable to a single
    nearly perpendicular pair. And <b>P</b> is strongly non-symmetric,
    |<b>P</b>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;<b>P</b><sup>T</sup>|<sub>max</sub>/|<b>P</b>|<sub>max</sub>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.72,
    as collocation is where Galerkin would not be. Uniqueness survives, needing
    definiteness and not symmetry, but this is a genuine LCP and not a convex
    QP, so the solver's convergence is an empirical fact here and is reported
    per run.
  </p>

  <h2>What interaction adds to screening everyone already does</h2>

  <p>
    Resolving Coulomb on each fracture independently against the regional
    stress &mdash; <b>critically-stressed fracture analysis</b>, after Barton,
    Zoback &amp; Moos (1995) &mdash; is routine in reservoir geomechanics and
    costs nothing. It is also exactly this study's no-interaction null. So the
    central measurement is the difference between that screening and the
    static solve, and the two halves of it disagree:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Membership</b>: the screening is close, but <i>how</i> close
      depends strongly on the stress orientation. Over the full grid of 12
      orientations &times; 25 frictions the Jaccard index with the static
      solve ranges <b>0.872 to 0.999</b>, median 0.986. Interaction adds up
      to <b>1,509</b> elements and removes up to 552.</li>
    <li><b>Moment</b>: the screening cannot speak to it at all, being a
      yes/no test with no slip. Interaction concentrates moment by a factor of
      about 2.2 relative to the footprint carrying it.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="note">
    <b>That orientation dependence was invisible from a single
    orientation, and an earlier version of this page reported only the
    single-orientation figure of "well under 3&nbsp;%".</b> The worst
    disagreement is at SHmax&nbsp;105&deg; (Jaccard 0.872, an eighth of the
    network), the best at SHmax&nbsp;0&deg; and 165&deg; (under 0.1&nbsp;%).
    So whether the standard screening is adequate is not a property of the
    network alone &mdash; it is a property of the network <i>and</i> the
    tectonic regime acting on it, which is precisely what the second slider
    exists to show.
  </div>

  <p>
    Because the solve is cheap, the network can also be <b>grown fault by
    fault</b> and re-solved, which a loading history could not afford. Leaving
    one fault out of the full network and warm-starting from its solution
    gives the clean perturbation, and <b>both signs occur</b>: removing one
    780-element fault switched 224 elements <i>off</i> and 12 on &mdash; it
    was mostly promoting its neighbours &mdash; while removing a 600-element
    fault switched 64 <i>on</i> and 8 off, having mostly been shadowing them.
    Removing an <i>inactive</i> fault, however large, changes nothing at all:
    a fault that does not slip transfers nothing, so the 1,444-element fault
    that dominates the locked set is mechanically invisible to the rest of the
    network.
  </p>

  <h2>Not every fault can ever slip</h2>

  <div class="note">
    <b>The measurements in this section come from a different configuration
    than the one displayed above</b> &mdash; 538 power-law faults swept in
    FRICTION rather than a subparallel cloud swept in LOAD. They are kept
    because the mechanics they describe is the same and the numbers were
    earned, but they are not statements about the cloud in the viewer, whose
    own event statistics are reported further up: median one patch, largest
    five, 7.9&nbsp;% multi-element.
  </div>

  <p>
    The load clamps as well as shears, so a plane's strength grows with
    <i>&lambda;</i> too. Failure needs
    <i>&lambda;</i>|<i>&tau;</i>|&nbsp;&ge;&nbsp;<i>&mu;</i>(<i>&sigma;<sub>c</sub></i>&nbsp;+&nbsp;<i>&lambda;&sigma;<sub>n</sub></i>),
    so an element with
    |<i>&tau;</i>|&nbsp;&le;&nbsp;<i>&mu;&sigma;<sub>n</sub></i> per unit load
    <b>never fails at any load whatsoever</b>. On the path shipped here that is
    <b>28.3&nbsp;%</b> of elements, so the active set has a ceiling of
    71.7&nbsp;% rather than 100&nbsp;%. The gate checks this the blunt way: a
    locked element's yield function is still negative at
    <i>&lambda;</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;10<sup>8</sup>. Elastic interaction is almost
    powerless against it &mdash; on the purely deviatoric path it pushed
    exactly <b>two</b> of 2,116 locked elements over.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Measured rather than argued.</b> At <i>k</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;1, where the
    load has no ceiling and <i>&lambda;</i>&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;&infin; is a
    legitimate question, a run of 1,021,035 events out to 20&times; the first
    failure reaches 47.5&nbsp;% active against a predicted ceiling of
    50.5&nbsp;% &mdash; 94&nbsp;% of the way there, with the remainder
    arriving logarithmically. The active set overlaps the no-interaction
    prediction by 99.3&nbsp;%. So the ceiling is real, interaction does not
    lift it, and the answer to whether everything eventually slips is no by
    roughly half.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>The active set is <i>almost</i> nested in <i>&mu;</i>, and the
    exceptions are real.</b> Raising friction can only shrink the screened
    set, by construction. With interaction it need not: LCP monotonicity
    requires <b>A</b> to be a <i>Z</i>-matrix &mdash; every off-diagonal
    coupling promoting &mdash; and the dislocation kernel has shadowing lobes
    too, with only 44&nbsp;% of pairs satisfying the condition here. So an
    element held locked at low <i>&mu;</i> by a neighbour's stress shadow can
    be <i>freed</i> at high <i>&mu;</i> when that neighbour stops slipping.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <b>An earlier version of this page said that escape is never taken. That
    was measured at one stress orientation and stated as though it held
    everywhere.</b> Across twelve orientations it is taken at three of them
    &mdash; 1 element at SHmax&nbsp;120&deg;, <b>142</b> at 105&deg;, 6 at
    165&deg;, and none at the other nine. The elements involved slip
    2.5&ndash;11.6&nbsp;m, so this is mechanics and not solver tolerance,
    whose floor is around 10<sup>&minus;8</sup>&nbsp;m. The prediction that
    non-monotonicity is <i>permitted</i> was right; the claim that it never
    happens came from a sample of one.
  </div>

  <p>
    Nothing downstream depends on it either way. The viewer reads membership
    from the per-frame slip array, which is exact whether or not the set
    nests; "critical friction" is reported as the <i>largest</i> <i>&mu;</i>
    at which an element slips, not as a guarantee that every lower one does.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>19.1&nbsp;% of the cloud slips</b> &mdash; 392 patches of 2,056 &mdash;
    by 2.3&nbsp;times the load at which the first one fails. Almost nothing is
    permanently locked (0.05&nbsp;%), so that fraction is not a ceiling; it is
    how far the load was taken. Events are small: the median is a single patch,
    the largest is five, and 7.9&nbsp;% involve more than one. The triggering
    budget is 0.63 &mdash; what one patch sheds onto others, as a fraction of
    what it sheds in total &mdash; so cascades die out, which is what
    subcritical means and why no event runs away.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>What the interaction adds is small, and the page shows exactly how
    small.</b> The honest comparison is not against nothing; it is against the
    set that would fail with the elastic coupling switched off, chosen by
    orientation and load alone. That null gets 98&nbsp;% of the same patches.
    Colour by <i>what interaction changed</i> and almost the whole cloud is
    grey: the loading does nearly all of the choosing, and the handful of
    patches interaction adds or removes is the entire mechanical contribution.
    Coherence &mdash; how nearly the slipping patches deform as one &mdash;
    moves from 0.785 to 0.800, about two per cent.
  </p>

  <p>
    What the active set does have is <b>shape</b>. Its clusters are strongly
    linear (0.91) and barely planar (0.03), against 0.87 and 0.07 for the
    no-interaction null. Subparallel planes scattered through a volume pick out
    <i>lineaments</i> rather than sheets when they slip together &mdash; which
    is the kinematically connected subset this configuration was built to look
    for, in a form that was not the one anticipated. Colour by
    <i>cluster planarity</i> to see it, and note how few patches are in a
    cluster larger than a handful: the largest holds eight.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the load is a full tensor and not a deviatoric one</h2>

  <div class="note">
    <b>An earlier version of this page was loaded past the bound below, and
    16.6&nbsp;% of its active set was failing for no mechanical reason.</b>
    The run shown now is compressive at every element in every frame, and the
    payload builder refuses to publish one that is not.
  </div>

  <p>
    A deviatoric <b>D</b> is traceless, so it raises the mean stress not at all:
    the tension-favoured planes shed normal stress exactly as fast as they gain
    shear. Past
    <i>&lambda;</i>&nbsp;&asymp;&nbsp;<i>&sigma;<sub>c</sub></i> their effective
    normal stress goes <i>negative</i>. Coulomb friction does not describe that
    &mdash; the fault opens &mdash; and worse, a negative
    <i>&sigma;</i><sub>eff</sub> makes the strength
    <i>&mu;&sigma;</i><sub>eff</sub> negative, so the element satisfies the
    yield criterion however little shear it carries and joins the active set
    for no mechanical reason.
  </p>

  <p>
    Purely deviatoric, the first element here opens at only 1.95&times; the
    first failure. Lowering <i>&sigma;<sub>c</sub></i> does not help, because
    first failure and opening scale with it identically &mdash; a lithostatic
    profile gives 2.15&times;. What helps is a loading path that also raises
    the mean stress, through the <i>k</i>&thinsp;<b>I</b> term:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th><i>k</i></th><th>the path</th><th>usable range</th>
      <th>locked</th><th>ceiling on active</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>0</td><td><i>&sigma;<sub>H</sub></i> up and
        <i>&sigma;<sub>h</sub></i> down equally; mean fixed</td>
        <td>1.95&times;</td><td>10.2&nbsp;%</td><td>89.8&nbsp;%</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>2/3</b></td><td><b>only <i>&sigma;<sub>H</sub></i> grows
        &mdash; shipped here</b></td><td><b>3.89&times;</b></td>
        <td><b>28.3&nbsp;%</b></td><td><b>71.7&nbsp;%</b></td></tr>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>minimum principal stress never falls</td>
        <td>unbounded</td><td>49.5&nbsp;%</td><td>50.5&nbsp;%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    <i>k</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;2/3 is not fitted to anything: holding
    <i>&sigma;<sub>h</sub></i> and <i>&sigma;<sub>v</sub></i> while
    <i>&sigma;<sub>H</sub></i> grows raises the mean stress at
    &Delta;/3 while the maximum shear rises at &Delta;/2. An isotropic tensor
    resolves the same normal stress onto every plane and no shear onto any, so
    <i>k</i> enters the arithmetic in exactly one place and every downstream
    formula is untouched &mdash; checked to machine precision rather than
    assumed.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>The more realistic the loading path, the fewer faults can ever
    slip.</b> That is the uncomfortable half of the table: going from a
    deviatoric load to one that never opens a fault cuts the ceiling from
    89.8&nbsp;% to 50.5&nbsp;%. The deviatoric case was flattering itself by
    driving a tenth of the network toward tension.
  </p>

  <p>
    None of the conclusions below depend on which of the three was used. At
    <i>k</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0 to 1.9&times; (the last compressive load), at
    <i>k</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;2/3 to 3.85&times; (shipped) and at
    <i>k</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;1, the overlap with the no-interaction null is
    97.1&nbsp;%, 97.9&nbsp;% and 99.0&nbsp;%; the participation ratio differs
    from that null by &minus;0.0003, +0.0012 and +0.0035; and the mean event
    size is 5.86, 5.83 and 5.90 against a largest of 845, 840 and 836. What
    moves is the active fraction &mdash; 51.9&nbsp;%, 52.8&nbsp;%,
    42.5&nbsp;% &mdash; and nothing else.
  </p>

  <h2>What is and is not being claimed</h2>

  <p>
    The machinery works and the numbers above are measured: 165,659 events over
    the load range shown, 52.8&nbsp;% of elements eventually slipping, a
    cumulative moment of 3.1&times;10<sup>21</sup>&nbsp;N&nbsp;m, and a largest
    single cascade of 840 elements. Every event reached equilibrium; none ran
    away, which is not automatic &mdash; the branching ratio averages 0.786 and
    its 90th percentile is 0.969, so this network sits just below criticality
    rather than comfortably away from it.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Interaction does not choose which elements slip. It chooses how much
    each one slips.</b> That is the result, and it is not what we expected to
    find. The active set overlaps the <i>no-interaction null</i> &mdash; the
    set that would fail if elements could not talk to each other, selected by
    orientation and the regional load alone &mdash; by <b>97.9&nbsp;%</b>. Hold
    the weighting fixed and every geometric statistic of the two sets agrees:
    coherence differs by +0.0005, the participation ratio by
    +0.0012, the orientation fabric's leading eigenvalue by +0.0009, cluster
    planarity and linearity to three decimals. The faults that fail are the
    well-oriented ones, and elasticity barely moves that list.
  </p>

  <p>
    What elasticity does move is the <i>distribution of slip across that
    list</i>. Weighted by moment rather than by membership, the active set's
    participation ratio falls from 0.378 to 0.172 &mdash; moment is about 2.2
    times more concentrated than the footprint carrying it &mdash; and its
    coherence rises from 0.731 to 0.780. So slip piles onto a mutually
    better-aligned subset of the elements that were always going to fail. The
    network manufactures asperities out of slip amplitude, not out of which
    patches participate.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <b>Two of those numbers were wrong here first, in the same way.</b> The
    build originally weighted the active set by real slip while weighting every
    null by unit slip, then printed the difference in one column. That read as
    interaction concentrating the active set by a factor of two and sharpening
    its coherence by +0.057. Both effects were the weighting. Each statistic is
    now computed at matched weighting across every row, and the moment-weighted
    comparison is made against the same set's own unit-weighted value rather
    than across rows &mdash; which is how a factor of 2.2 in moment survived as
    real while the +0.057 became &minus;0.003.
  </div>

  <p>
    <b>The dimension question cannot be asked in this domain at all.</b> The
    box-counting window is bounded below by the <i>largest fault</i>, not the
    element size, because until a box exceeds a fault that fault reads locally
    two-dimensional. With a 37.9&nbsp;km fault in a 100&nbsp;km box the window
    spans <i>zero</i> decades, and the code refuses to report a slope rather
    than fitting one. Measuring <i>D</i><sub>active</sub> against
    <i>D</i><sub>geom</sub> needs either a domain of order 1,400&nbsp;km or a
    network whose largest fault is a small fraction of the box; the
    multiplicative-cascade configuration exists for exactly that and keeps its
    largest fault at 1.6&nbsp;% of the domain against 38&nbsp;% here.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two standing limits on what a dimension could ever say. The estimator is
    biased low by about 0.11 and that bias is <i>invisible to
    R<sup>2</sup></i> &mdash; every calibration fit reaches
    R<sup>2</sup>&nbsp;&ge;&nbsp;0.999, including one wrong by 0.14 &mdash; so
    only differences in a matched window are quoted. And a subset's dimension
    falls simply because it has fewer members: on a sheet that is exactly
    two-dimensional, a 5&nbsp;% random subset reads 0.36 lower and a 1&nbsp;%
    subset loses a full dimension. So &ldquo;the active set is
    lower-dimensional&rdquo; is nearly free, and the matched-cardinality null
    is the entire result rather than a formality.
  </p>

  <h2>Avalanches</h2>

  <div class="note">
    <b>The measurements in this section come from a different configuration
    than the one displayed above</b> &mdash; 538 power-law faults swept in
    FRICTION rather than a subparallel cloud swept in LOAD. They are kept
    because the mechanics they describe is the same and the numbers were
    earned, but they are not statements about the cloud in the viewer, whose
    own event statistics are reported further up: median one patch, largest
    five, 7.9&nbsp;% multi-element.
  </div>

  <p>
    An event here is one relaxation to equilibrium after a single element is
    pushed past its static strength &mdash; a cascade through the network, not
    a dynamic rupture, and nothing in it propagates in time. Sizes are broad:
    58.5&nbsp;% involve more than one element, the 99th percentile is 71
    elements and the largest is 840, against a median of 2.
  </p>

  <p>
    No exponent is quoted. A power law needs a defended lower cutoff, and these
    sizes are bounded above by the elements per fault &mdash; 38.6 on average
    here &mdash; so a fitted slope would be reporting the discretisation as
    much as the network. The survival function is given instead:
    <i>P</i>(&ge;2)&nbsp;=&nbsp;58.5&nbsp;%,
    <i>P</i>(&ge;5)&nbsp;=&nbsp;23.9&nbsp;%,
    <i>P</i>(&ge;10)&nbsp;=&nbsp;12.3&nbsp;%,
    <i>P</i>(&ge;50)&nbsp;=&nbsp;1.9&nbsp;%,
    <i>P</i>(&ge;100)&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.5&nbsp;%. An earlier configuration with
    small faults made this point the hard way: its event size was invariant to
    a 16&times; density change and a 25&times; change in the strength drop,
    because it was set by the discretisation within a fault rather than by the
    network &mdash; which is Rice's discreteness critique arriving uninvited.
  </p>

  <h2>The stress isosurface, and why this cloud has none yet</h2>

  <p>
    The viewer can raymarch a translucent isosurface of the elastic maximum
    shear stress &mdash; an invariant,
    (&sigma;<sub>1</sub>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;&sigma;<sub>3</sub>)/2, so it does
    not depend on a reference plane the way a Coulomb stress change would
    &mdash; with a threshold slider moving the shell. <b>The control is absent
    above because this network's volume has not been evaluated.</b> The page
    renders whatever the payload carries and omits the toggle when there is no
    volume, rather than offering a control that fails on click.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>The reason is cost, and the cost was first estimated wrong by a factor
    of six.</b> Evaluating the field costs one kernel pair per grid point per
    element, and a stress readout parallelises at only 1.45&times; here &mdash;
    it is the <i>assembly</i> of an interaction matrix that parallelises at
    8.5&times;, a distinction worth keeping straight, because reading the wrong
    row is what produced the original estimate. Measured end to end at 54.1
    &micro;s/pair, a 24<sup>3</sup> grid against all 2,056 elements is about
    4.3 hours. The previous configuration's volume took two.
  </p>

  <p>
    A twenty-times-cheaper route was tried and rejected on measurement.
    Replacing each fault by its own rectangle carrying the same total moment
    &mdash; geometry exact, moment exact, only the slip distribution within a
    fault averaged &mdash; agrees with the exact field to a median of
    0.00&nbsp;% on a coarse grid, correlation 0.9997. But resolved against
    distance from the nearest fault it is wrong by a median 3.8&nbsp;% and a
    worst 38&nbsp;% within one fault length, falling below 0.1&nbsp;% only
    beyond about eight. An isosurface at a high threshold selects precisely the
    near-fault points, so the approximation is worst exactly where the picture
    is most interesting. Any volume published here is therefore exact over
    every element, and a coarse grid is the price: at 4.2&nbsp;km cells against
    2&ndash;38&nbsp;km faults it would show the network's <i>aggregate</i>
    stress structure, not a concentration at a fault tip.
  </p>

  <h2>Why there is a strength drop</h2>

  <p>
    With a single friction coefficient this experiment has no mechanism,
    because friction that does not weaken cannot go unstable. A patch unloads
    itself elastically at a stiffness <i>k</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;|<i>K<sub>ii</sub></i>|,
    and instability requires the strength to fall with slip faster than that
    &mdash; the classical criterion <i>k</i>&nbsp;&lt;&nbsp;|d&tau;/d&delta;|.
    Non-weakening Coulomb friction has d&tau;/d&delta;&nbsp;=&nbsp;0, so the
    inequality can never be satisfied at any patch size, stress or stiffness.
  </p>

  <p>
    The consequence is that the quasi-static solution is <b>continuous</b> in
    the load. Beyond failure, equilibrium pins the traction to the strength, so
    slip follows
    &delta;(&lambda;)&nbsp;=&nbsp;(&lambda;&thinsp;&tau;'&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;&tau;<sub>p</sub>)/|<i>K<sub>ii</sub></i>|
    &mdash; it grows from <i>zero slip</i> at a <i>finite</i> rate
    &tau;'/|<i>K<sub>ii</sub></i>|. Nothing jumps, so no event has a finite
    size and there is nothing to count. The first version of the solver
    demonstrated exactly that, advancing the load to the first failure and then
    relaxing zero slip indefinitely.
  </p>

  <p>
    So an element has a static strength
    &tau;<sub>p</sub>&nbsp;=&nbsp;&mu;<sub>s</sub>&sigma;'<sub>n</sub> it
    must reach to fail and a residual strength
    &tau;<sub>r</sub>&nbsp;=&nbsp;&mu;<sub>d</sub>&sigma;'<sub>n</sub> it
    relaxes down to. The drop
    &Delta;&tau;&nbsp;=&nbsp;&tau;<sub>p</sub>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;&tau;<sub>r</sub>
    forces a finite slip
    &Delta;&delta;&nbsp;=&nbsp;&Delta;&tau;/|<i>K<sub>ii</sub></i>| the instant
    an element fails, and that slip is what loads its neighbours and can
    trigger them in turn. This is slip-weakening in the limit of zero
    weakening distance, so the nucleation length collapses to zero and every
    element is unstable at any size &mdash; the price of not resolving a
    breakdown zone. Here
    &mu;<sub>s</sub>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.6 and
    &mu;<sub>d</sub>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.54 against 150&nbsp;MPa give
    90&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;81&nbsp;MPa, a <b>9&nbsp;MPa stress drop</b>, which sits
    inside the 1&ndash;10&nbsp;MPa range observed for real earthquakes. At a
    ratio of 1.0 there are no cascades by construction, and the code refuses
    that value rather than returning a null result that would look like a
    finding.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <b>It does not follow that the drop sets the avalanche statistics, and
    measurement says it does not.</b> A failing element slips
    &Delta;&tau;/|<i>K<sub>ii</sub></i>| and delivers
    <i>K<sub>ji</sub></i>&Delta;&tau;/|<i>K<sub>ii</sub></i>| to its neighbour,
    while the gap that neighbour must re-close after relocking is itself
    proportional to &Delta;&tau; &mdash; so the drop cancels from the
    triggering ratio, which is pure geometry. Measured: a <b>25&times; change
    in the drop moved mean avalanche size by under 2&nbsp;%</b>, with
    events&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;drop constant to 9&nbsp;%. What the drop does set
    is whether events exist at all, the moment carried by each, and how many
    there are &mdash; not how large they are in elements, which is most of what
    this page measures.
  </div>

  <h2>What the solver choice costs</h2>

  <p>
    A cascade has no unique end state &mdash; a triggered element lands anywhere
    between its dynamic and static strength depending on the order elements are
    visited &mdash; so there is no exact answer to validate against. What can be
    done is to vary the update rule and measure the spread. Measured on the
    smaller cascade configuration rather than on this network: between
    Gauss-Seidel and Jacobi, event counts move by 6.9&nbsp;% and mean event
    size by 4.3&nbsp;%; between element orderings, under 1&nbsp;%. But total
    slip is invariant to 0.1&nbsp;%, and the <b>active set came out bit
    identical</b> under every rule tried &mdash; symmetric difference zero. So
    the set every claim above is made on carries no solver dependence, while an
    avalanche exponent would carry that 7&nbsp;% as part of its uncertainty
    &mdash; one of several reasons none is quoted. The same sweep has not been
    repeated at 2,056 elements, where each relaxation is a hundred times more
    expensive.
  </p>

  <h2>One simplification worth naming</h2>

  <p>
    Effective normal stress is <b>uniform with depth</b> here, and that is a
    deliberate choice rather than an oversight. The real gradient is about
    0.0167&nbsp;GPa/km once hydrostatic pore pressure is removed, which across
    a 40&nbsp;km box is a factor of twenty in strength: the shallow elements
    would fail first, the active set would be a horizontal slab, and its
    measured dimension would be reporting the depth gradient rather than any
    mechanical localisation. Removing it leaves orientation and elastic
    interaction as the only things that can select which faults slip, which is
    the question. The depth-dependent case runs, and is important future work
    &mdash; it needs its own null, the same network with the gradient and the
    elastic interaction switched off, or depth and localisation stay confounded.
  </p>

  <p class="cap">
    Slip is stored quantised: log<sub>10</sub> into one byte per element per
    frame, 254 levels, zero reserved for &ldquo;has never slipped&rdquo; and
    drawn grey rather than at the bottom of the ramp, because a locked element
    is categorically different from one that slipped a little. The whole
    120&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;2,056 array is encoded in one pass, so the colour
    scale is global across frames &mdash; a per-frame scale would normalise away
    the growth being animated. The quantisation error is bounded by arithmetic
    rather than asserted, and the build measures what it wrote against that
    bound: 1.239&nbsp;% against a bound of 1.239&nbsp;%, a ratio of 1.000. The
    payload is 0.55&nbsp;MiB for 2,056 elements across 120 equilibria, plus two single-frame arrays that describe the final state rather than its history.
  </p>

